                <pb facs="0223" n="119" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg119"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Mizner</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-10-21"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 21,
                        1890</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 197.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose a copy of two dispatches from the
                    United States vice-consul at Tegucigalpa, by which it appears that,
                    notwithstanding your telegram of the 19th ultimo to the vice-consul, mercantile
                    correspondence by means of the cable via La Libertad has continued interrupted
                    since the cessation of hostilities. You will make most earnest representations
                    against the prolongation of a state of things so injurious to friendly
                    commercial relations.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James G. Blaine.</hi></signed></closer>







